{{also|Riddle}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɹɪdəl/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪdəl|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|redel}}, {{m|enm|redels}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rǣdels}}, {{m|ang|rǣdelse|t=counsel, opinion, imagination, riddle}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*rēdisliją|t=counsel, conjecture}}. Analyzable as {{af|en|rede|t1=advice|-le}}. Akin to {{cog|osx|rādisli}}, {{m|osx|rādislo}}, {{m|osx|rēdilsa}} ({{cog|nds|Radels}}, {{cog|nl|raadsel}}), {{cog|goh|rātisla}} ({{cog|de|Rätsel||riddle}}), {{cog|ang|rǣdan||to read, advise, interpret}}.

====Noun====
{{examples-right|: '''6th c. {{BCE}}''', Laozi, ''{{w|Tao Te Ching}}'' 9:
:: Keep sharpening the blade, you'll soon blunt it.
: {{RQ:King James Version|Luke|17|33}}
:: Whosoever shall seek to save his life shall lose it; and whosoever shall lose his life shall preserve it.
|sense=ancient form}}
{{en-noun}}

# A [[verbal]] [[puzzle]], [[mystery]], or other [[problem]] of an [[intellectual]] nature.
#: {{syn|en|enigma|conundrum|brain-teaser}}
#: {{ux|en|Here's a '''riddle''': It's black, and white, and red all over. What is it?}}
#* {{w|John Milton}} (1608-1674)
#*: To wring from me, and tell to them, my secret, / That solved the '''riddle''' which I had proposed.
#* {{RQ:Chmbrs YngrSt|VIII}}
#*: Elbows almost touching they leaned at ease, idly reading the almost obliterated lines engraved there. &para; {{nowrap|"I never}} understood it," she observed, lightly scornful. "What occult meaning has a sun-dial for the spooney? ''I'm'' sure I don't want to read '''riddles''' in a strange gentleman's optics."
# An ancient verbal, poetic, or literary form, in which, rather than a [[rhyme]] scheme, there are parallel opposing expressions with a hidden meaning.

